(f) `make check` (vet, static, vuln, css-verify, full `go test ./...`) → all
  packages `ok`, ends "✓ all checks passed". `gofmt -l` on every changed file →
  clean. `go build ./...` / `go vet ./...` → clean.

Attacks investigated and REFUTED with evidence (not just eyeballed):
- Aliasing: `p.form` and `p.request` are both value-typed
  `SpendingOptimizerRequest` copies; `p.request` is later overwritten with
  `retained.Request` (line 304) via a JSON round trip, `p.form` is never
  reassigned after construction — confirmed by reading the full handler and
  by the poison-value test in (a).
- Clone/DeepCopy path (`prepare.Clone`/`DeepCopy`): both go through
  `json.Marshal`/`Unmarshal`; `SpendingSearch` has a normal json tag (not
  `json:"-"`), so it round-trips correctly without needing hand-written
  carry logic — verified `carryJSONOmittedFields` only touches
  CurrentAge/SpouseAge, and `go test ./internal/services/retirement/prepare`
  passed inside `make check`.
- Field-by-field reconstruction sites that could silently drop the new
  field: grepped every `WhatIfSettings{` literal outside models/prepare
  (only `prepare.go:95`, which is fed by DeepCopy's JSON output, not a
  literal enumeration) and every settings-mutating handler
  (`UpdateSettings`, `UpdateSettingsWithPersons`, `handleWhatIfGuardrails`,
  `cloneForCurrentMonth`) — all load-then-mutate-named-fields, never
  rebuild the struct, so `SpendingSearch` cannot be lost. No MCP
  `apply_changes`/scenario-copy code constructs `WhatIfSettings{}` either.
  Live-tested two of these paths in (e).
  `CloneSpendingSearchPreferences` is added but genuinely unused anywhere
  in the tree (grep confirmed) — harmless defensive helper, not a sign of a
  missed call site, since (unlike LivingSpendingBoost) SpendingSearch never
  flows through the optimizer's in-memory candidate machinery that needs
  clone-on-mutate protection.
- Whole-cent money vs `%.2f` display: `analysis.NormalizeSpendingRequest`
  rejects non-cent SearchMin/Max/StepMonthlyReal (spending_optimizer.go:306-311)
  during the search itself; since Apply is only reachable after a
  successful search populated `p.tokens`, the raw values ever saved into
  SpendingSearch are already cent-exact by construction — a non-cent
  value can never reach Apply, so the `%.2f` render can't disagree with a
  later re-submission.
- `step="0.1"` / float formatting: wrote a throwaway probe
  (`strconv.FormatFloat(i/10.0,'f',-1,64)` for i in 0..999) — zero
  mismatches and zero cases with >1 fractional digit, so no restored
  shortfall value can trip `stepMismatch` for a value the UI itself could
  have produced. (Probe discarded per calibration rule; not kept as a
  test.)
- `{{with}}` zero-is-falsy semantics for `NearTermYearsSaved` (int) and the
  three money fields (float64) confirmed correct per Go template `IsTrue`
  and by the "none"/"strict-zero" test cases rendering blank.
- `{{if $form.Chained}}` guards the entire form including the new inputs;
  since `spendingOptimizerFormData` populates the new keys unconditionally,
  a chained scenario hides the block without any missing-key template
  panic (verified by reading the template guard structure).

Findings for backlog (out of scope for SP1, not grounds for FAIL):
- `QualificationRule` on the results view formats `MaxShortfallPct` with
  `%.1f` (pre-existing, untouched by this diff) while the restored form
  uses `FormatFloat(...,-1,...)` (exact). For a shortfall value with more
  than one decimal (only reachable via a direct POST bypassing the
  browser's `step="0.1"`, e.g. 12.34), the results sentence would show
  "12.3%" while the reloaded form would show "12.34" — a pre-existing
  dual-formatter mismatch unrelated to the restore bug SP1 fixes, and not
  introduced by this change.

No FAIL found. Every criterion (a)-(f) has direct command/log evidence above.
